WHY A PROPRIETARY LICENCE ON A PORTFOLIO REPOSITORY

This is not a defensive habit; it follows from what this code does. The server
drives an authenticated session against a third-party job platform, and that
platform's terms of service prohibit automated access. A permissive licence
would invite a stranger to run it against their own account, or against
somebody else's, with this repository's name attached to whatever followed.

The specific hazard here is sharper than in the sibling servers, and it is
stated where a reader meets it rather than buried in a tool docstring:
`uplers_apply` expresses interest, which on Uplers IS applying, and it CANNOT
BE UNDONE. There is no withdraw, no cancel and no un-apply anywhere in the
product -- verified as a complete negative search over the vendor's entire
frontend bundle. The only thing that retracts an application is deactivating
the whole account. A reader who copies this code and points it at their own
account can do something to their own job search that nobody can reverse.

So: a portfolio artifact meant to be READ, not deployed.

THIS IS NOT AN OPEN SOURCE LICENCE. It grants nothing. If you want to do
something with this code, ask.
